---
import PageLayout from "@layouts/PageLayout.astro"
import TopLayout from "@layouts/TopLayout.astro"
import BottomLayout from "@layouts/BottomLayout.astro"
import { ABOUT } from "@consts"
---

<PageLayout title={ABOUT.TITLE} description={ABOUT.DESCRIPTION}>
  <TopLayout>
    <div class="animate page-heading">
      {ABOUT.TITLE}
    </div>
  </TopLayout>
  <BottomLayout>
    <div class="animate">
      <article>

        <p>
          I'm Zijian, an MSc Quantitative Finance and FinTech student at The Hong Kong
          Polytechnic University, preparing for PhD research on <b>AI decision systems
          under uncertainty</b>.
        </p>

        <p>
          My undergraduate training at the University of Melbourne combined <b>finance,
          economics, and mathematics</b>. That background shaped the way I approach AI
          research: I am interested not only in whether a model predicts well, but in
          whether it can support decisions when signals are noisy, incentives are complex,
          and feedback arrives late.
        </p>

        <h2>The Research Through-line</h2>

        <p>
          The question I keep returning to is how intelligent systems should observe,
          represent, decide, and adapt. In finance, this appears as forecasting, ranking,
          portfolio construction, leakage control, and risk-aware evaluation. In recommender
          systems, it appears as sparse feature interaction and sequential user behavior.
          In robotics and agentic AI, it becomes state estimation, planning, memory, and
          action under physical or operational constraints.
        </p>

        <p>
          I see these areas as different laboratories for the same deeper problem:
          reliable decision-making under uncertainty. Finance is my current testbed because
          it is data-rich, time-dependent, and unforgiving about validation discipline.
          But the technical direction is broader than finance alone.
        </p>

        <h2>Systems as Thinking Infrastructure</h2>

        <p>
          I have spent years building personal knowledge systems with tools like
          <b>Obsidian</b> and <b>Notion</b>. I do not treat them as aesthetic dashboards.
          I treat them as <b>thinking infrastructure</b>.
        </p>

        <p>
          Research ideas rarely arrive fully formed. They begin as fragments: paper notes,
          project logs, experiment observations, failed hypotheses, and questions that are
          not useful yet. A good system helps me:
        </p>

        <ul>
          <li>separate observations from conclusions;</li>
          <li>track why an experiment failed;</li>
          <li>connect concepts across finance, AI, and robotics;</li>
          <li>preserve questions that may become future research ideas;</li>
          <li>review long-term goals without losing sight of weekly execution.</li>
        </ul>

        <p>
          The point is not to become more busy. The point is to become more
          <b>coherent</b>: to preserve the reasoning trail behind the work and make future
          ideas easier to develop honestly.
        </p>

        <p>
          This is also how I approach agentic AI. I prefer to begin with a real task,
          solve it manually, and observe where retrieval, tool use, state, memory,
          evaluation, guardrails, and cost control become necessary. I expand this
          problem-first approach in <a href="/blog/ai-agent-native-learning-system/">AI-Agent
          Native Learning</a>.
        </p>

        <h2>Sport and Research Discipline</h2>

        <p>
          I train consistently through <b>tennis</b> and <b>Hyrox-style conditioning</b>.
          Sport gives feedback that is difficult to avoid. If my movement is late, the
          ball is gone. If my conditioning is weak, the workout exposes it. If I become
          emotional, my execution gets worse.
        </p>

        <p>
          That feedback loop matters for research temperament. A model fails. A result
          looks promising and then collapses under a better validation split. An experiment
          log contradicts the story I wanted to tell. The useful response is not panic.
          It is <b>diagnosis</b>, followed by another careful attempt.
        </p>

        <h2>History, Philosophy, and Long-term Thinking</h2>

        <p>
          Outside technical work, I'm drawn to <b>Chinese history and philosophy</b>.
          Part of the attraction is scale. Historical thinking makes individual problems
          feel less absolute. It reminds me that systems, incentives, institutions, and
          human behavior interact over long periods.
        </p>

        <p>
          That perspective matters for AI and finance. Models don't exist in empty space.
          They operate inside social, economic, and institutional environments. A technically
          impressive system can still fail if it ignores incentives, feedback loops, or
          human behavior.
        </p>

        <p>
          Philosophy also helps me think about ambition. I want to work seriously, but not
          frantically. I want to build competence over years, not only chase short bursts of
          performance.
        </p>

        <h2>Why This Matters</h2>

        <p>
          A PhD is not only a test of whether someone can understand difficult ideas. It
          is also a test of whether they can return to a problem again and again, organize
          uncertainty, recover from failed attempts, and keep improving without needing
          immediate reward.
        </p>

        <p>
          My systems, sport, and humanities interests all point toward the same underlying trait:
          I care about <b>sustained development</b>. I'm learning to become someone who can hold
          a difficult question for a long time, keep records honestly, train consistently, and
          return to the work with more clarity each time.
        </p>

        <p>
          That, to me, is part of becoming a researcher.
        </p>

      </article>

      <div class="mt-12 pt-6 border-t border-black/10 dark:border-white/25">
        <a href="/" class="group flex gap-1.5 items-center text-sm hover:text-black dark:hover:text-white transition-colors duration-300 ease-in-out">
          <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="stroke-current">
            <line x1="19" y1="12" x2="5" y2="12" class="scale-x-0 group-hover:scale-x-100 translate-x-3 group-hover:translate-x-0 transition-all duration-300 ease-in-out" />
            <polyline points="12 19 5 12 12 5" class="translate-x-1 group-hover:translate-x-0 transition-all duration-300 ease-in-out" />
          </svg>
          <span>Back to home</span>
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </BottomLayout>
</PageLayout>
